  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Make the square coefficients one",
      "body": "<p>Divide the whole equation by 2 before comparing it with the usual circle form. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A circle equation is easiest to read after the squared terms have coefficient one.",
      "pitfall": "Divide the constant term too.",
      "pause": "Complete the square in x and in y separately.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l13",
          "reminder": "Put the circle equation into centre-and-radius form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Distinguish radius from radius squared",
      "body": "<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, the right-hand constant is the square of the radius. The radius is its positive square root.</p>",
      "recap": "Read the geometric quantity requested, not just the constant you obtained.",
      "pitfall": "A negative radius is not a second possibility.",
      "pause": "Take the positive square root and match the options.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l13",
          "reminder": "The standard circle form gives the centre and radius squared."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Dividing by 2 and completing both squares gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The radius is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer B.</strong></p>",
